CAT = "ieqfaEthics"; SEG = "ETH"
Q = [
("An adviser earns a higher commission on Product X than on an equally suitable Product Y. The ethical course is to:",
 "Recommend on suitability, not pay", ["Recommend X quietly", "Recommend X and waive half the fee", "Let the client toss a coin"],
 "Commission must not impair the duty to act honestly, fairly and professionally in the client's best interests. The recommendation must rest on the client's needs, and remuneration must be disclosed."),
("Under the Code, a firm that both charges a client a fee and receives commission must explain:",
 "If commission is offset against the fee", ["How commission is spent", "The salaries of its other advisers", "Which insurer pays the most"],
 "The Code requires a firm to explain, before charging any fee, whether the commission will be offset against the fee, in part or in full."),
("Under the Code, a regulated firm may pay commission for regulated activities to:",
 "Another regulated entity", ["Any person who refers a client", "A client's relative as a thank-you", "An unregistered introducer abroad"],
 "Commission, fees or rewards may be paid only to regulated entities, certified persons and the other categories the Code lists, such as tied individuals a firm takes full responsibility for."),
("A conflict of interest arises when:",
 "A firm's own interests could sway advice", ["A client disagrees with a fee quote", "Two clients live at the same address", "An adviser changes to a new employer in the sector"],
 "A conflict exists where the firm's or adviser's own interests, or another client's, could compete with the duty owed to the client. Firms must identify, manage and, where needed, disclose conflicts."),
("A conflict of interest cannot be managed away. Before acting, the firm should at least:",
 "Disclose it clearly to the client", ["Note it on the file", "Ask the client to sign a blank form", "Mention it after the sale is closed"],
 "Disclosure is the last resort, not a cure-all: the client must be told enough, before acting, to decide whether to proceed. If the conflict harms the client, the firm should decline."),
("A product provider offers a broker's adviser a free weekend abroad if she meets a sales target. The adviser should:",
 "Decline it as an improper inducement", ["Accept it if the clients never find out", "Accept it and sell only that product", "Share it with her best clients"],
 "Gifts and benefits that could influence advice conflict with acting in the client's best interests. Firms keep gifts policies, and minor hospitality must not affect recommendations."),
("An adviser is asked about a complex pension transfer that is outside her competence. She should:",
 "Refer it to a qualified colleague", ["Give her best guess to keep the client", "Tell the client to search online", "Copy advice given to another client"],
 "Acting with due skill, care and diligence means staying within your competence and referring matters you are not qualified to handle."),
("A client wants to cancel a life policy to buy a new one. Before arranging the replacement, the adviser must:",
 "Show the client what would be lost", ["Cancel the old policy at once", "Sell the new one without further comment", "Tell the old insurer to refuse"],
 "Replacing a policy can cost the client lost benefits, new exclusions, higher premiums or fresh charges. The adviser must compare the policies and explain any loss before acting."),
("An adviser moves a client between funds every few months, earning a fee each time, though the client's goals have not changed. The main ethical problem is that:",
 "His own income comes first", ["The client earns too much", "The funds are all regulated", "The fees are paid by direct debit"],
 "Switching to generate fees rather than to meet the client's needs is churning, a breach of the duty to act in the client's best interests."),
("A friend asks an adviser at a social event what a mutual acquaintance has invested. The adviser should:",
 "Say nothing about it", ["Give a rough figure only", "Answer if the friend is discreet", "Check the file first, then share"],
 "Client information is confidential and protected by data protection law. It may be disclosed only with consent or where the law requires."),
("An employee reports suspected wrongdoing at her firm to the Central Bank. Which law protects her from penalisation?",
 "The Protected Disclosures Act 2014", ["The Consumer Credit Act 1995", "The Equal Status Acts", "The Investor Compensation Act 1998 as amended"],
 "The Protected Disclosures Act 2014, as amended in 2022, protects workers who report relevant wrongdoing, and the Central Bank is a prescribed person for regulated firms."),
("A person in a pre-approval controlled function learns of a serious breach of financial services law at the firm. Under the Central Bank (Supervision and Enforcement) Act 2013, they must:",
 "Report it to the Central Bank", ["Wait until the next annual audit", "Raise it only with the shareholders", "Report it only if asked by a client"],
 "Section 38 of the 2013 Act places a duty on PCF holders to disclose to the Central Bank information about breaches of financial services legislation, as soon as practicable."),
("An adviser's sister wants to buy an income protection policy through her. The adviser should:",
 "Apply the full suitability process", ["Skip the fact-find to save time", "Give her the firm's best price only", "Sell her whatever she asks for"],
 "Family and friends are consumers like anyone else: the knowing the consumer and suitability rules apply, and the personal relationship is a conflict to manage."),
("An adviser discovers she gave a client a wrong figure that affects his decision. The ethical response is to:",
 "Correct it promptly with him", ["Hope the client does not notice", "Correct it quietly at the next annual review", "Blame the product provider"],
 "Honesty and integrity require prompt correction, and the Code's errors rules require firms to put errors right and notify affected consumers."),
("Which practice breaches the Code's rules on informing customers effectively?",
 "Hiding key exclusions in small print", ["Explaining all the technical terms in plain English", "Giving a summary of key features", "Answering questions in writing"],
 "Firms must not present key information in a way that disguises, diminishes or obscures its importance."),
("An adviser pressures an elderly client to sign the same day, saying the offer ends tonight when it does not. This is:",
 "Misleading, high-pressure selling", ["Normal and acceptable practice", "Fine as long as the product is suitable", "Allowed for existing customers"],
 "False urgency misleads the client and is especially harmful to consumers in vulnerable circumstances, whom the Code requires firms to assist."),
("A firm's ethics policy says advisers should treat customers fairly. In practice this means the adviser:",
 "Puts the client's interests first", ["Offers every client the same product", "Gives all clients identical advice", "Charges every client the lowest fee"],
 "Fair treatment is about outcomes suited to each client's needs, not identical treatment. The Standards for Business require acting in customers' best interests."),
("Under the Common Conduct Standards, an individual must deal with the Central Bank:",
 "In good faith and without delay", ["Only through the firm's lawyers", "Only when an issue is resolved", "Only by written correspondence"],
 "Section 53E of the Central Bank Reform Act 2010 sets the Common Conduct Standards, one of which is to cooperate in good faith and without delay with the Central Bank, including by answering its requests promptly and never giving it false or misleading information."),
("A broker realises a client's details on an application are wrong because he misheard them. He should:",
 "Fix it with the client and insurer", ["Leave it, since the client signed", "Change it on the form without telling the client", "Wait to see whether a claim arises"],
 "An uncorrected error could lead to a reduced or refused claim. Due skill and care means fixing it openly with both the client and the insurer."),
("A client of a bank's tied adviser asks whether the adviser can recommend products from other providers. The honest answer explains:",
 "Which providers the adviser acts for", ["That every adviser covers the whole market", "That other providers are all unsafe", "That the question is confidential"],
 "Clients must be told the firm's regulatory status and the range of providers it deals with, so they understand the basis of the advice."),
("An adviser is offered a share of a solicitor's fee for referring clients to her. The main ethical risk is:",
 "An undisclosed conflict of interest", ["A breach of copyright law", "A loss of the client's data", "An income tax charge on the referral fee"],
 "Undisclosed referral payments can sway recommendations. Any such arrangement must be assessed as a conflict and disclosed, and may be prohibited if it impairs the duty to the client."),
("A motor insurer's claims handler is offered free servicing by a garage that the handler sends repair work to. The handler should:",
 "Decline and tell the compliance team", ["Accept it if the repairs are good", "Accept it and send more work there", "Accept it but keep it off the file"],
 "A personal benefit from a supplier the handler chooses creates a conflict of interest that could harm claimants. It should be declined and reported under the firm's policy."),
("A client tells an adviser in confidence that he plans to understate his income on a mortgage application. The adviser should:",
 "Refuse to submit a false application", ["Submit it, since it is the client's choice", "Lower the figure a little further", "Ask the lender not to check it"],
 "Knowingly submitting false information is dishonest and may be fraud. The adviser must refuse, and should consider their reporting obligations."),
("A firm offers bonuses based only on the number of policies its advisers sell. The main conduct risk is:",
 "Pressure to sell unsuitable products", ["Advisers working too few hours", "Clients paying lower premiums", "Fewer complaints being received by the firm"],
 "Volume-based incentives can reward mis-selling. Firms must ensure remuneration does not impair the duty to act in customers' best interests."),
]
